      "writtenOn": "2024-01-30T19:36:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Manipulating the state of the fpsensor task \n\nI was thinking about this more when looking at `fpsensor_before` in https://crrev.com/c/5234757.\n\nWe have to do things like:\n\n```\n\t/* Clear MKBP events from previous tests. */\n\tfp_get_next_event((uint8_t *)\u0026fp_events);\n```\n\nand \n\n```\n\tzassert_ok(ec_cmd_fp_mode(NULL, \u0026params, \u0026response));\n\tzassert_equal(response.mode, 0);\n```\n\nto reset the fpsensor task from the test.\n\nWhat if we refactor the fpsensor task a bit so that it exposes an API to reset its internal state? That would avoid \"leaking\" the state outside of the fpsensor task and probably make it easier if we add more state to the fpsensor task in the future. (Just update the \"reset internal state\" function rather than update all the tests.) I\u0027m not sure how easy this would be given that the code in the fpsensor task is not great. At one point we tried to pull out all the stateful code/variables into `fpsensor_state.cc`, so some of the groundwork may be there. Just something to think about as you\u0027re looking at this code.",
      "parentUuid": "f0ddb2b0_7769f05a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 337,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 349,
        "endChar": 3
      },
      "revId": "8e9f2f94a1e3a112b9f45b73db9bfd6a669a4923",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8d75c7c8_b155ec5a",
        "filename": "zephyr/test/fingerprint/task/src/fpsensor_capture.c",
        "patchSetId": 22
      },
      "lineNbr": 349,
      "author": {
        "id": 1441652
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-30T20:35:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e What if we refactor the fpsensor task a bit so that it exposes an API to reset its internal state?\n\nI have an idea to rewrite fpsensor logic to use the same state machine framework that TCPMv2 use. If we implement it correctly, we will only need to switch the state to the beginning.\n\nI believe that changing mode to 0 should be sufficient to reset the state. If it\u0027s not, we should fix the fpsensor code. Regarding MKBP events. In some tests we are not reading the MKBP event, because the test focuses on different properties.",
      "parentUuid": "c374b263_1fd66648",
